<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter10_operator-overloading-and-class-interfaces">
    <title>Operator Overloading and Class Interfaces</title>

    <p>
        Built-in types already have useful operators. For example, C++ knows
        how to add two integers with <c>+</c> and compare two integers with
        <c>==</c>. A class can give selected operators meaningful behavior for
        its own objects. This is called <term>operator overloading</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Operator overloading does not create a new kind of operator. It defines
        a function whose name contains the operator symbol, such as
        <c>operator+</c> or <c>operator==</c>. The familiar expression remains
        readable while the class supplies the behavior.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-overloading-purpose">
        <title>Operators Can Express Natural Class Operations</title>

        <p>
            Consider a <c>Point</c> object with horizontal and vertical
            coordinates. Adding two points can naturally mean adding their
            corresponding coordinates. Equality can naturally mean that both
            coordinates match.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_point-operators">
            <title>Overloading addition and equality for <c>Point</c></title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_point-operators"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Point {
private:
    int x;
    int y;

public:
    Point(int point_x, int point_y)
        : x{point_x}, y{point_y} {
    }

    int get_x() const {
        return x;
    }

    int get_y() const {
        return y;
    }

    Point operator+(const Point& other) const {
        return Point{x + other.x, y + other.y};
    }

    bool operator==(const Point& other) const {
        return x == other.x && y == other.y;
    }
};

int main() {
    Point first{2, 3};
    Point second{4, 1};
    Point total = first + second;

    std::cout << total.get_x() << ", " << total.get_y() << '\n';
    std::cout << (first == second) << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The expression <c>first + second</c> calls
            <c>first.operator+(second)</c>. The expression
            <c>first == second</c> calls <c>first.operator==(second)</c>.
            The operator notation is shorter, but the member-function meaning
            is still present.
        </p>

        <p>
            An overloaded operator should match the meaning that readers would
            reasonably expect. If an operation does not have a clear natural
            interpretation, a named member function is usually a better
            interface.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-overloading-forms">
        <title>Member and Non-Member Operator Functions</title>

        <p>
            An operator can be implemented as a member function or as a
            non-member function. The choice depends on the operation and on
            which operands need access to the class interface.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_operator-function-forms">
            <title>Two common forms of overloaded operators</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="27%"/>
                <col width="29%"/>
                <col width="24%"/>
                <col width="20%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Form</cell>
                    <cell>Example declaration</cell>
                    <cell>Typical operands</cell>
                    <cell>Useful when</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Member operator</cell>
                    <cell><c>Point operator+(const Point&amp;) const;</c></cell>
                    <cell>The left operand is a <c>Point</c>.</cell>
                    <cell>The operation naturally belongs to the left object.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Non-member operator</cell>
                    <cell><c>operator&lt;&lt;(std::ostream&amp;, const Point&amp;)</c></cell>
                    <cell>The left operand is not a <c>Point</c>.</cell>
                    <cell>The operation must work with another type first.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            A member operator receives the left operand through <c>this</c> and
            receives the other operand as a parameter. That is why the member
            declaration for <c>operator+</c> has one explicit parameter even
            though the expression has two operands.
        </p>

        <p>
            A non-member operator receives all of its operands as parameters.
            This is important for stream output. In
            <c>std::cout &lt;&lt; point</c>, the left operand is an
            <c>std::ostream</c>, not a <c>Point</c>, so the output operator is
            naturally a non-member function.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-output-stream">
        <title>Overloading <c>operator&lt;&lt;</c> for Output</title>

        <p>
            A user-defined object does not automatically know how to work with
            <c>std::cout</c>. We can define a non-member
            <c>operator&lt;&lt;</c> that describes its text representation.
            Returning the stream by reference allows output expressions to be
            chained.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_point-output">
            <title>Giving <c>Point</c> a stream-output interface</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_point-output"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Point {
private:
    int x;
    int y;

public:
    Point(int point_x, int point_y)
        : x{point_x}, y{point_y} {
    }

    int get_x() const {
        return x;
    }

    int get_y() const {
        return y;
    }
};

std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& output, const Point& point) {
    output << '(' << point.get_x() << ", " << point.get_y() << ')';
    return output;
}

int main() {
    Point point{3, 4};

    std::cout << "Point: " << point << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The function uses the public accessor functions instead of reading
            private data directly. This keeps the class interface in control of
            how its state is observed. A friend output function could access
            private members directly, but friendship should be used only when
            that closer relationship is justified.
        </p>

        <p>
            The return type is <c>std::ostream&amp;</c>, and the function returns
            <c>output</c>. This is what makes an expression such as
            <c>std::cout &lt;&lt; point &lt;&lt; '\n';</c> possible: the first
            operation returns the stream for the next operation.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-to-string">
        <title>A <c>to_string()</c> Function for Text Representation</title>

        <p>
            A class can also provide a <c>to_string()</c> member function that
            returns a <c>std::string</c> representation of the object. This is
            useful when code needs text but is not writing directly to a stream.
            The stream-output operator can then delegate to the same function
            instead of duplicating the formatting logic.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter10_point-to-string">
            <title>Using <c>to_string()</c> inside <c>operator&lt;&lt;</c></title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter10_point-to-string"
                     language="cpp"
                     interactive="yes"
                     line-numbers="yes">
                <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

class Point {
private:
    int x;
    int y;

public:
    Point(int point_x, int point_y)
        : x{point_x}, y{point_y} {
    }

    std::string to_string() const {
        return std::string{"("}
             + std::to_string(x)
             + ", "
             + std::to_string(y)
             + ")";
    }
};

std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& output, const Point& point) {
    return output << point.to_string();
}

int main() {
    Point point{3, 4};

    std::string description = point.to_string();
    std::cout << "Point: " << description << '\n';
    std::cout << "Again: " << point << '\n';

    return 0;
}
                ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            The class's <c>to_string()</c> function is a user-defined member
            function. It uses <c>std::to_string()</c> to convert the integer
            coordinates into text. Calling <c>std::to_string(point)</c> would
            not automatically work because the standard function does not know
            how to format an arbitrary <c>Point</c> object.
        </p>

        <p>
            Keeping the formatting in one place makes the interface easier to
            maintain. If the desired point format changes, the class can update
            <c>to_string()</c>, and the stream operator will use the updated
            representation automatically.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-overloading-rules">
        <title>Rules and Design Limits</title>

        <p>
            Operator overloading has important limits. It changes how an
            operator works with a user-defined type, but it does not let a
            programmer redesign the C++ language.
        </p>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter10_operator-overloading-rules">
            <title>Important operator-overloading rules</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="42%"/>
                <col width="58%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell>Rule</cell>
                    <cell>Meaning</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>At least one operand must be a user-defined type.</cell>
                    <cell>A class cannot redefine the meaning of <c>int + int</c> for the whole program.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The operator's precedence does not change.</cell>
                    <cell>Overloading <c>+</c> does not make it bind more tightly or loosely.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>The number of operands does not change.</cell>
                    <cell>A unary operator remains unary, and a binary operator remains binary.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>New operator symbols cannot be invented.</cell>
                    <cell>Only existing C++ operators can be overloaded.</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Meaning should remain understandable.</cell>
                    <cell>Use a named function when operator notation would confuse readers.</cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            Operator overloading is therefore part of a class's public design.
            A good overload makes an object easier to use without hiding an
            unexpected side effect. A poor overload can make ordinary-looking
            code difficult to understand.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter10_operator-overloading-codelens">
        <title>Tracing an Overloaded Operator</title>

        <p>
            Step through the expression <c>first + second</c> and observe the
            new <c>Point</c> object returned by <c>operator+</c>.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter10_operator-overloading"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Point%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20int%20x%3B%0A%20%20%20%20int%20y%3B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Point%28int%20point_x%2C%20int%20point_y%29%20%3A%20x%7Bpoint_x%7D%2C%20y%7Bpoint_y%7D%20%7B%7D%0A%20%20%20%20Point%20operator%2B%28const%20Point%26%20other%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20return%20Point%7Bx%20%2B%20other.x%2C%20y%20%2B%20other.y%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%20%20%20%20int%20get_x%28%29%20const%20%7B%20return%20x%3B%20%7D%0A%20%20%20%20int%20get_y%28%29%20const%20%7B%20return%20y%3B%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Point%20first%7B2%2C%203%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Point%20second%7B4%2C%201%7D%3B%0A%20%20%20%20Point%20total%20%3D%20first%20%2B%20second%3B%0A%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20total.get_x%28%29%20%3C%3C%20%22%2C%20%22%20%3C%3C%20total.get_y%28%29%20%3C%3C%20%27%5Cn%27%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=560&amp;codeDivWidth=450&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;mode=display&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="4:3">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter10_operator_exercise-purpose">
        <statement>
            <p>What does operator overloading provide for a class?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>A class-specific meaning for an existing C++ operator.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The operator remains a C++ operator, but the class supplies behavior for its objects.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A way to invent entirely new operator symbols.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Only existing C++ operators may be overloaded.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A way to change operator precedence throughout the program.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Overloading does not change precedence.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>A way to make every class member public.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Operator overloading and access control are separate features.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_operator_exercise-member">
        <statement>
            <p>In the expression <c>first + second</c>, what does a member <c>operator+</c> use as its explicit parameter?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>The right-hand <c>Point</c>, while <c>first</c> is represented by <c>this</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A member function receives the left object implicitly and the other operand explicitly.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Both <c>first</c> and <c>second</c> as two explicit parameters.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The left operand is the object that calls the member function.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Only the output stream.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>An output stream belongs to <c>operator&lt;&lt;</c>, not this addition operation.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>No operand because overloaded operators cannot have parameters.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The other operand must still be supplied.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_operator_parsons">
        <statement>
            <p>Arrange the lines to define a member addition operator that returns a new point.</p>
        </statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>Point operator+(const Point&amp; other) const {</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>    return Point{x + other.x, y + other.y};</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>}</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_operator_exercise-stream-return">
        <statement>
            <p>Why does a stream-output operator return <c>std::ostream&amp;</c>?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>Returning the stream allows output operations to be chained.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The next <c>&lt;&lt;</c> operation can use the stream returned by the previous one.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It creates a new stream for every object.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The existing stream is returned by reference.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It converts every object into a string automatically.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The function writes a representation to the stream; it does not necessarily create a string.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>It makes the class inherit from <c>std::ostream</c>.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>The class and the stream remain separate types.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter10_operator_exercise-limits">
        <statement>
            <p>Which statement is a valid limitation of operator overloading?</p>
        </statement>
        <choices>
            <choice correct="yes">
                <statement><p>At least one operand must have a user-defined type.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>A class cannot globally redefine an expression such as <c>int + int</c>.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Only arithmetic operators can be overloaded.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Comparison and stream operators are also common overloads.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Every overloaded operator must be a static function.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Operators can be member or non-member functions, depending on the design.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
            <choice>
                <statement><p>Overloading automatically changes the operator's precedence.</p></statement>
                <feedback><p>Precedence remains part of the C++ language rules.</p></feedback>
            </choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

</section>
